# Aerial2Mbtiles

Converts georeferenced archival aerial photographs into MBTiles ready for use in mobile map viewers, while normalizing file names for long-term archiving.

The project was created to process a library of German archival aerial photographs from World War II. Each photograph is manually georeferenced in Google Earth and saved either as a KMZ archive or as a raster + KML pair; this tool automates the rest of the pipeline.

## What it does

For each input (a KMZ archive or a raster + KML pair), the pipeline:

1. Resolves the input into a concrete raster + KML pair. For a KMZ archive, the archive is unpacked into a temporary directory using the same entry-name encoding as Google Earth (code page 437 with UTF-8 override).

2. Shows the four current names found in the input: the raster file name, the KML file name, the KML `<name>`, and the KML <href>.

3. Asks for a normalized master name (pre-filled with the raster file name; Enter accepts the suggestion) and for a processing mode (1 or 2).

4. Normalizes the pair into a {master} directory: the raster is copied under {master}{ext}, the KML is patched so that `<name>` is {master} and <href> is {master}{ext}, and the binding is rewritten as gx:LatLonQuad (from either gx:LatLonQuad or LatLonBox with optional rotation, computed on the WGS84 ellipsoid).

5. In mode 2, additionally creates a GCP-tagged GeoTIFF in EPSG:4326 from the four corners, reprojects to EPSG:3857, converts to MBTiles with PNG tiles, and builds overviews for smooth zooming on mobile devices.

KMZ archives are no longer written to the output: the normalized pair is the canonical archived form.

## Requirements

Windows x64

- GDAL command-line tools on PATH (gdal_translate, gdalwarp, gdaladdo, gdalinfo)

Python 3 (only for the verification script)

The published executable is self-contained and does not require a .NET runtime on the target machine.

## Usage

Drag and drop onto Aerial2Mbtiles.bat:

one .kmz file, or

a raster image and a .kml file (in any order).

Or run the executable directly:
    Or run the executable directly:

Aerial2Mbtiles.exe "path\to\file.kmz"
Aerial2Mbtiles.exe "path\to\image.tif" "path\to\overlay.kml"
, the tool prints the four current names, asks for the master name (Enter accepts the suggested value) and for the processing mode (Enter accepts mode 2).

## Modes

- Mode 1 — normalize only. Produces a {master} directory with the normalized pair. No MBTiles.

- Mode 2 — normalize + MBTiles. Produces the same {master} directory and additionally writes {master}.mbtiles inside it.

## Output

Results are written next to the input, inside a {master} directory:

{master}/
├── {master}{ext} — normalized raster (copied byte-for-byte)
├── {master}.kml — KML with synchronized `<name>`, <href> and binding
└── {master}.mbtiles — mode 2 only

## Supported inputs

- KMZ produced by Google Earth: doc.kml with a <GroundOverlay>, and a files/ folder with exactly one raster.

Pair: a raster image and a .kml file. Raster extensions recognized: .jpg, .jpeg, .tif, .tiff, .png.

## Supported binding styles

gx:LatLonQuad — four corner coordinates.

LatLonBox with optional rotation — converted to four corners on the WGS84 ellipsoid using Vincenty's formulae. Rotation is applied in the local metric plane, then projected back to geographic coordinates.

On output, the binding is always written as gx:LatLonQuad.

## Verification

compare_mbtiles.py compares two MBTiles files by the SHA-256 hashes of their tiles and reports any difference, per zoom level.

Drag and drop two .mbtiles files onto compare_mbtiles.bat, or run from a console:
    or run from a console:

compare_mbtiles.bat reference.mbtiles output.mbtiles
 and is intended as a manual verification tool, not as part of the conversion pipeline.

## Published files

A release publish produces a single zip archive (Aerial2Mbtiles-<version>-win-x64.zip) containing five files:
    containing five files:

Aerial2Mbtiles.exe — self-contained executable (Windows x64)
Aerial2Mbtiles.bat — drag-and-drop launcher for conversion
compare_mbtiles.py — MBTiles verification script
compare_mbtiles.bat — drag-and-drop launcher for comparison
e_sqlite3.dll — native SQLite library (required at runtime)

src/
├── Aerial2Mbtiles.Cli/ — executable, console UI, batch launcher, verification script
└── Aerial2Mbtiles.Core/ — conversion pipeline, GDAL wrapper, naming, KML/ZIP helpers

